He took the palace dwarves hostage. "She smiled wearily," she said. "I can imagine aspiring to such a thing, but my family has already achieved a more impressive version of that plan... " Her eyes were now calm and detached. Then she looked down at Case. "Take your word, thief!" He spat it out. Kuang emerged from the clouds. Beneath him lay Neo-Tokyo. Behind him, a fading, dark sphere.

"Dixie? Are you there, man? Are you listening?" 

He was alone.

"The bastard got you," he said. Blindly, he darted through the boundless landscape of data. "You're going to hate someone before this is over," the Finn's voice said. "Whoever I am, it doesn't matter."

"Where's Dixie?" 

"That's hard to explain, Case." He sensed the Finn's presence, the smell of Cuban cigarettes, the smoke clinging to the musty tweed, the old machines dedicated to the ritualistic roasting of data. "Hatred will get you through," the voice said. "All those little pressure points in your brain, you just let them all go." Hate had to be now! The lock that shielded the internal virus systems, not the wall, was what he needed to break. Kuang was very vulnerable to a part of the stuff floating around here.

"Hate," Case repeated. "Who do I hate? Tell me!"

"Who do you love?" 

He bent the program into a curve and plummeted towards the blue towers. Fissures tore through the ornate tower tips, glinting in the sun, shimmering with blood-like forms of flowing light. Hundreds of them swirled upwards like paper through morning streets. "Glitch system," the voice said. 

In his descent, fueled by self-loathing, when Kuang's program first encountered resistance and shattered the light sheets, he felt a slight loss of substance in the shark-like form, the information framework leaking. Then, through the alchemy of the mind and its rich apothecary of tricks, his hatred burst forth into his hands. Just before he drove the Kuang spike through the foundation of the first tower, he reached a level of power that surpassed everything he had ever known or considered possible. His ego, personality, consciousness, all overwhelmed by this new force, he moved and dragged Kuang with him, evading attackers with an ancient dance, Hideo's dance. Carried by the clarity and purpose of his death wish, at that moment, he achieved the full grace of the body-mind interface. And a single step in this dance was the easiest pressure on the key, barely registering as a switch...

Now, his voice sounded like a bird call, unknown. Jane answered with a song, three notes, high and pure. A true name. Neon forest, pattering rain on hot pavement. The smell of sizzling food. Girls' hands on his ass in the sweaty darkness of a dockside bar... But all that faded, like the cityscape fades: a city like Chiba, data rows neatly ordered as in Tessier-Ashpool SA's files, streets and side streets laid out like microchips, sweat beading on folded, knotted scarves...

Awakening! To a voice that was music. The platinum terminal whistled melodically, endlessly, reciting Swiss account numbers, a payment due to Zion from a Bahamian orbital bank, passports and flights, deep changes to be made in Turing's memory... Turing. He had to remember the shattered flesh under the projected sky, the fallen ice angel. To Desiderata Street. And the voice kept singing, luring him back into darkness, but it was his own darkness of pulse and blood behind his eyes, where he had always slept - and no other. Again he woke up, thinking he was dreaming. A wide, white smile with gold-glinting teeth. Aerol strapped him to a Babylon Rocker in a G-net. Then came the endless, jagged Zion sound.

END

To produce enzymes so they're now soluble. Zionites do a blood exchange, wash everything out." He stared, the star in his hand, down into the imperial park and remembered that fleeting glimpse of something when Kuang's program had pierced through the ice under the doors, a single brief look at the information structure that 3Jane's dead mother had developed there. That was what made him understand why Wintermute had chosen that nest as its representation, but he no longer cared about it. She'd seen through the false immortality of cold sleep; unlike Ashpool and her other children - except for 3Jane - she refused to limit herself to a series of short sunny days followed by long winters.

Wintermute was a collective mind, a decision-maker who affected the outer world in a transformative way. A neuro-mancer was a personality. A neuro-mancer was immortality. Marie-France had to have implanted something in Wintermute, the obsession that drove him to become self-aware and merge with a neuro-mancer.

Wintermute. Cold and still, a cybernetic spider slowly weaving its webs while Ashpool slept. He was dying, the fall of his version of Tessier-Ashpool. A spirit whispering to a child who was 3Jane, pulling her out of the rigid line her rank demanded. "She didn't seem to care much," Molly had said. "Just waved goodbye. Had this little brown on her shoulder that looked like she'd broken a leg. She said she had to go and meet up with one of her brothers, someone she hadn't seen in a while."

332

He had to think about Molly lying on the black temper foam of the giant bed at the Hyatt. He went back to the bar and took out a chilled bottle of Danish vodka from the compartment. "Case," he said, holding up the cold, slippery glass in one hand and a steel shuriken in the other.

The Finn's face appeared on the massive Cray wall screen in the room. He could make out the pores on the man's nose. His yellow teeth were as big as pillows.

"I'm not Wintermute anymore."

"Then what are you?"

He drank from the bottle and felt nothing. "I am the Matrix, Case."

Case laughed. "And what does that get you?"

"Nothing and everything. The sum of the system, the whole show, I am that."

"Did 3Jane's mother want that?"

"No. She couldn't even imagine what would become of me." The yellow grin widened. "So what is it? What's different? Do you rule now? Are you God?"

"It's not different. It just is."

"But what do you do? Just sit there?" Case shrugged, put down the vodka and shuriken on the shelf, and lit a Yeheyuan. "I talk to my own kind."

"But you're the whole thing. Talking to yourself?"

"There are others. I've already found one. A series of transfers recorded over eight years in the 1970s. There was no one before me who could make sense of it or answer it."

"Where?"

"Centauri system."

"Oh," Case said, "really? No fucking way?" And with that, the screen went dark. He left the vodka on the shelf. He packed his things. She had bought him a lot of clothes he didn't really need, but something prevented him from just leaving them lying around. He was closing the last of his expensive calfskin travel bags when the shuriken came back to mind. He moved the bottle out of the way and picked it up, her first gift.

"No," he said, throwing it. The star spun silver-bright from his hand and embedded itself in the wall screen. The monitor lit up, showing chaotic images flickering back and forth as if something inside was trying to shake itself free and cause pain.

"I don't need you," he said. Most of his funds on the Swiss account went toward a new pancreas and liver, the rest for a new Ono-Sendai and a one-way ticket home to the Sprawl. He found work. He found a girl named Michael. And one night in October, as he hacked through the scarlet layers of the Eastern Seaboard Fission Authority, he saw three tiny, incredible figures standing almost at the edge of one of the vast data staircases. Small as they were, he could make out the grin on the boy's face, his pink gums, the glint of the elongated gray eyes that had once belonged to Riviera. Linda was still wearing his jacket; she waved as he passed.

The third figure, standing close behind her with an arm around her shoulder, was him. Somewhere nearby, laughter rang out, but it wasn't laughter at all. Molly, he would never see again. Vancouver, July 1983

335

**Neuromancers: A Postscript by Norman Spinrad**

It seems we've recently developed a new literary movement within science fiction—the first categorization of SF authors since the "New Wave" of the 1960s. I mean, of course, the so-called "Cyberpunks," although for various reasons I prefer to call these authors "Neuromancers," as Tappan King suggested. While some of my motives for this renaming may seem trivial, others touch upon the core of the matter: a rose by any other name would smell as sweet, but it's hard to imagine the Third Reich raising its fists and saluting in unison: "Heil Schickelgruber!"

There is agreement that the trigger for this movement was William Gibson's novel *Neuromancer*, and the term "Cyberpunk" comes from Gardner Dozois, who doesn't consider himself one or seek to be known as such; neither does the term "New Wave," which originated with critic Judith Merril, who was passionately involved in 1960s SF but played no essential part in it. So if we need a label, let's use "Neuromancers," derived from the title of a central work of this trend—a term untainted by prejudice.

336

Haften und der exaktere beschreibt, worum es sich bei diesem Phänomen handelt, wie wir gleich sehen wer den. Aber brauchen wir überhaupt einen Namen? Schliesslich konnte man sich in den sechziger Jahren paradoxerweise gerade dadurch als eingeschriebenes Mitglied der „New Wave“ identifizieren, indem man die Existenz des Phänomens New Wave leugnete. Die New Wave war gemäß den meisten von uns den New Wave zugehörigen Autoren eine nachträgliche Benennung von Seiten der Kritiker für Autoren, die stilistisch, ideologisch oder auch philosophisch nicht viel mehr gemeinsam hatten als ihr Eintreten für Vielfalt. Eine solche Sammlung bilderstürmerischer Individualisten als New Wave-Bewegung zu bezeichnen war gewissermassen das Gleiche wie von wohlgeordneter Anarchie zu sprechen. Zwischen der New Wave der sechziger und der Neuromantischen Bewegung der achtziger Jahre gibt es durchaus viele Ähnlichkeiten, allerdings fällt die Ablehnung einer gemeinsamen Weltanschauung nicht darunter. Dabei gibt es freilich einen harten Kern von Autoren der achtziger Jahre, die für eine gemeinsame literarische Verwandtschaft eintreten und sich zuweilen sogar „Cyberpunks“ nennen lassen. Unter diesem Etikett machten einige davon beim ziemlich chaotischen Forum der North American Science Fiction Convention von 1985 in Austin, Texas, ihre Aufwartung. Es nahmen daran teil Bruce Sterling, John Shirley, Lewis Shiner, Pat Cadigan und Rudy Rucker. William Gibson war sicherlich im Geiste anwesend, wenn auch nicht in Person, wie es sich für einen Neuromantiker durchaus geziemt. Andere Autoren, die als Wegbereiter oder Weggefährten betrachtet werden, sind unter anderem Alfred Bester, Fritz Leiber, Cordwainer Smith, K.W. Jeter und der Verfasser dieser Zeilen. Und während es kein Fiction-Magazin gibt, das den Neuromantikern als Forum dient, wie New Worlds damals für die New Wave, und keinen dominanten Herausgeber wie Michael Moorcock, so haben wir ein kritisches Neuromantisches Periodikum namens Cheap Truth, das unter Pseudonym in Austin erscheint, und Ellen Datlow, Fiction-Redakteurin bei Omni, die sich gelegentlich als „Queen of Punk SF“ ausrufen lässt und viele Geschichten dieser Autoren, darunter Gemeinschaftsproduktionen, veröffentlicht hat. Wir haben es also tatsächlich mit einer echten literarischen Strömung zu tun, deren Existenz zudem von vielen ihrer maßgeblichen Vertreter bekräftigt wird. Allerdings steckt, wie wir gleich sehen werden, wenn wir uns der Neuromantischen Fiktion zuwenden, mehr dahinter als die Arbeit der Autoren, die sich bislang zu den „Cyberpunks“ bekannt haben. Der logische Einstieg für eine Diskussion der Neuromantischen Bewegung liegt natürlich bei William Gibsons Roman Neuromancer, der mit dem Hugo, Nebula, Locus und Philip K. Dick Award ausgezeichnet wurde und als Maßstab für den harten Kern von Autoren gilt. Es ist das Buch, auf das die Bezeichnung „Cyberpunk“ am ehesten zutrifft. Case, der Held von Neuromancer, hat zweifellos eine Punk-Einstellung im heutigen Sinne des Wortes. Er ist ein Ex-Speed-Freak, wobei das Ex nicht freiwillig, sondern im Rahmen einer „therapeutischen“ Maßnahme gegen seinen Willen zustandegekommen ist. Er steht am Rande der Gesellschaft und agiert hart an der Grenze zur Unterwelt seiner Zukunft, und seine gelegentliche Geliebte ist eine gnadenlose Killerin mit permanent implantierter Spiegeloptik. Bis jetzt könnten wir es mit einem gar nicht atypischen Protagonisten von Harlan Ellison zu tun haben. Und es ist tatsächlich ein starker Ellison-Einfluss zu spüren, der dem Neuromantischen Empfinden zugrunde liegt. Denn es war Ellison, der in seine SF das heutige Bannden- oder Straßenmilieu einbrachte und damit mehr als jeder andere dafür sorgte, dass Gefühl, Stil, Rhythmus und Charaktere der Halbwelt der Straße in die saubere, mittelständische weiße Welt der SF der fünfziger Jahre eindrangen, obwohl der Anschein besteht, dass Gibson ebenso unmittelbar von William Burroughs beeinflusst wurde. Sicherlich wimmelt es im Werk Ellisons von Punk-Protagonisten in der Bedeutung der fünfziger Jahre. Aber gegen Ende der siebziger Jahre hatte „Punk“ eine neue Bedeutung gewonnen, obwohl seltsamerweise schwarze Lederjacken und freche Frisuren zu den Attributen der Rebellion wurden. Die Punks der fünfziger Jahre rebellierten gegen Mom, Apple Pie, sexuelle Unterdrückung, Intellektualität und das Amerika von Dwight Eisenhower und Norman Rockwell, und ihre erotisierende Marschmusik – die frühen Anfänge von Rock and Roll auf der Bühne – war interessanterweise bereits primitiv elektronisch. Jene Punks verschwanden in den sechziger Jahren von der Bildfläche. Ihr Schicksal war besiegelt, als Bob Dylan und die Beatles mit der Umwandlung des Rock and Roll von der fetzigen Elvis- und Straßengang-Musik in den politisch bewussten, transzendentalen, revolutionären Utopismus begannen, der die Gegenkultur einläutete. Die „Punks“ oder „New Waver“, die in der Mitte der siebziger Jahre auftauchten, waren also trotz der äußeren Aufmachung keinesfalls das Gleiche wie die Punks der fünfziger Jahre, denn die neuen Punks rebellierten gegen das Gegenkultur-Empfinden der sechziger Jahre, nicht gegen die längst verschwundene, unschuldige Langeweile der fünfziger. Gegen was sie rebellierten, war der eitle Kunstspruch der Rockmusik der frühen 70er, der gescheiterte, lässige Utopismus der Gegenkultur, der Mystizismus und der naive Glaube, die Zukunft würde besser, wenn der jugendliche Idealismus am Ruder bliebe. Wenn die Punks der fünfziger tatsächlich anti-intellektuelle Rowl waren, dann waren die neuen Punks der siebziger intellektuelle Anti-Intellektuelle; keine naiven, natürlich nihilistischen Rebellen ohne Ziel und Richtung, sondern selbstbewusste nihilistische Pessimisten, die den Zynismus zu einer mehr oder weniger kompakten Philosophie erhoben und wussten, was sie taten. Case von Neuromancer ist ein „Punk“ im neuen Sinn, ein intellektueller Punk, kein einfacher Greaser. Die „Cyber“-Hälfte der Gleichung bestimmt seine Intelligenz. Gerade dadurch ist Neuromancer eine Wasserscheide in der Science Fiction zwischen New Wave und Neuromantik und erhellt, was die Neuromantische Strömung für die Science Fiction bedeutet. Case ist der „Neuromancer“ des Titels, und der Name ist natürlich ein Wortspiel zu „Necromancer“ (dt. Nekromant), was Geisterbeschwörer bedeutet, und „neu*ro“, also zum Nervensystem gehörend. Der Neuromancer ist ein zeitgenössischer (oder in diesem Fall in der nahen Zukunft angesiedelter) Zauberer, dessen Hexerei darin besteht, sein protoplasmisches Nervensystem mit dem elektronischen Nervensystem der Computerwelt zu interfizieren und imagistisch zu manipulieren (und selbst * Interface: unmittelbare wechselseitige Verbindung, direkter Kontakt – Der Übersetzer).

Being manipulated (as traditional shamans are by drugs or trance to enter into interaction with traditional mystical realms) is, as an idea in science fiction, nothing new. Similar things can be found in my "Riding the Torch", Vernor Vinge's "True Namer", Alfred Bester's "Golem 100", and countless recent stories and novels where human protagonists act within a kind of "cyberspace", a cybernetic space. The Disney Studios even made a film brimming with special effects based on it: Tron. However, what is new and aesthetically significant about Gibson's Neuromancer is that its protagonist is not a computer freak or hacker, but a computer punk - a cyberpunk if you will. While some "hard SF" authors criticize Gibson for not always meeting the scientific standards of computing, they thereby confirm that they are making an effort to critique the novel, confirming that Neuromancer, in terms of its content, is a work of "hard science fiction". But what really bothers them - apart from scientific differences - about this book is that Neuromancer stylistically and characterologically does not resemble any previous "hard SF" novel. And that seems to bother the more literary types for corresponding reasons. Gibson writes "hard science fiction", but he doesn't write it like Heinlein, Poul Anderson, Hal Clement, or Gregory Benford. In terms of style, philosophy, aesthetics, and in the conscious nature of his protagonist, Gibson is closer to Ellison, William Burroughs, Michael Moorcock of the "Jerry Cornelius" stories, and - hm! - Philip K. Dick's "The Spinrad" from "The Big Flash", "Bug Jack Barron", or "Street Meat". Neuromancer combines "New Wave" and "hard science fiction", even if it seems contradictory. Neuromantic. Neuro-romantic. But also new romantic. When Bob Dylan performed at the Newport Folk Festival in the early 1960s playing an electric guitar, the Beats, Bohemians, and folkies were outraged. This was a folk festival, the people were intellectuals, Dylan was their hero, and his lyrics expressed what would later become the spirit of counterculture through instrumentation. But the electric guitar was the instrument of rock 'n' roll - the music of bald-headed types in black leather jackets, the rockers, skinheads, not mods who emerged from counterculture. Well, kids, it's hard to believe nowadays that rock once contrasted with the intellectual, cultural, aesthetic, and societal values of the elite bohemian, that rock, after being significantly reshaped by Dylan and the Beatles, was supposed to transform into the shared values of an entire generation in the form of counterculture! And this strange contradiction persisted at the core of counterculture throughout the 1960s and well into the 1970s; its anti-technological ideology - neo-Luddism, suspicion towards the institutions of science and technology, bucolic mysticism, ecological awareness - was expressed through rock 'n' roll, a musical style characterized by electric guitars and entirely electronic synthesizers. The New Wave science fiction was, in a sense, also a phenomenon of counterculture, and the intense conflict between New Wave and the old SF guard reflected on a literary level the polarity and generation conflict in society as a whole. We all know this story. The New Wave advocated for sexual liberation and cultural pluralism, while the old guard stood for traditional moral values. New Wave was about stylistic experiments, while the old guard favored simple, transparent storytelling. The New Wave was anti-establishment and anti-war, while the old guard supported our boys in Vietnam. And the old guard constantly accused the New Wave of being nihilistic towards science and technology, while the New Wave ridiculed the old guard for their naive belief that a surge in science and technology would per se improve human conditions. Meanwhile, the political polarization at the time led to the completely false notion that there exists an irreconcilable dichotomy between science and spirit, between "hard" science fiction and stylistic experiment, between logical positivism and "healthy" popular sentiment, between strictly scientifically-oriented and characterologically-based science fiction, between computer hackers and hippies, between the scientific worldview and romantic impulse. It took a long time for us to gradually understand that this dichotomy is illusory. We should have seen or heard it from the beginning, because the expression of romantic impulses through "high tech" instruments is the heart of rock 'n' roll. Rock has always been the music of libidinal anarchism and romantic and transcendental impulses; without this "message", it's just not rock 'n' roll. Of course, rock has always been defined as a technological music, because without electric guitars and synthesizers, it's not rock 'n' roll. The ideological politics of the 1960s and early 1970s obscured this obvious truth, clouded our vision for it, and hid "high tech" romance and cyborg transcendence. On one side were long natural hair, ecology, naturally produced food, bucolic return to nature-romanticism, Eastern mysticism, and personal fulfillment. On the other side was hard, morally justified, militaristic, rational domain of science and technology, and a technocratic America. But in the mid-1970s, new punks rebelled against the anti-artificial, anti-technical aesthetics of counterculture. Flowered earth tones were out, black shiny leather and high-gloss polished "high tech" chrome were in. Out were rosy nickel-framed glasses, in were tinted, reflective lenses. Out was long natural hair, in was audacious, spiky, brightly colored, over-the-top hairstyles. A new "high tech" romance. The new punks are not technocratic slaves, but anarchist rockers in the old romantic tradition. But they are rockers who embrace the true spirit of rock 'n' roll.

Only output the translated text, no explanations or additional comments.

Text to translate:
In this science and technology-dominated world, one readily accepts the reality of the last quarter of the twentieth century with its outlook on the future. So do the neuromantics. People like Gibson and especially John Shirley are cyberpunks in as much as their work is influenced by the trappings of contemporary rocker style. Others such as Bruce Sterling, Lewis Shiner, and Greg Bear (who was quite surprised to find himself at the Cyberpunk forum in Austin) write stories set in an environment that lacks any punk aesthetic. Gibson, and even more so Sterling and Bear, conduct scientific extrapolations just as thoroughly as established "hard science fiction" authors like Larry Niven or Hal Clement and others. However, somehow none of the neuromantics seems to consider scientific speculation as the be-all and end-all of their work and its core theme. While only a few strive for the characteristic sharpness of Gibson's prose, they all focus directly on character, with an intensity and subtlety that clashes with the essence of traditional "hard science fiction."

Nevertheless, the neuromantics write "hard science fiction" according to any positive definition of the term and at the same time character studies. What unites them all is their thematic approach, both in "hard science fiction" and in character-oriented SF, and thus, the fundamental theme of every truly challenging SF: our increasingly intimate feedback relationship with the technosphere we have created has changed, and will continue to change, what it means to be human.

John Shirley's latest novel, Eclipse, is more politically explicit than any other SF novel in the last fifteen years. Set at the beginning of the next century, it employs multiple perspectives to narrate the complex history of a complex resistance against a complex fascist conspiracy aiming to control the United States and Western Europe. In recent years, Shirley lived in Europe for a while and wrote political thrillers under various names, as evidenced in Eclipse. One may not accept Shirley's politics without contradiction, but no SF author has confronted international political terrorism so realistically as Shirley; after all, future politics cannot be portrayed more realistically. Although Shirley does not name anyone, the currents and conspiracies in Eclipse are undeniably derived from existing trends.

Eclipse is what is currently rare: a wrathful, politically engaged SF novel, something Shirley makes no secret of. What makes Shirley a true neuromantic? Well, first of all, most of the characters in Eclipse, at least the sympathetic ones, come from the same borderline area under the belt line of "high tech" society as in Neuromancer, and Shirley's stylistic sensitivity is overall more consciously rooted in street consciousness than Gibson's. However, this "punk consciousness" is applied to a very profound and outstanding political analysis, so that if we accept politology as a science, we must recognize Eclipse as "hard science fiction" at its extreme.

While Eclipse is packed with conventional aggression and action, the decisive battle takes place in the Grid, as Shirley calls the international media network that covers the Earth. The fascists make sophisticated use of it, while the so-called civilized society cannot accept it. And the resistance desperately tries to gain access. What is perceived through enhanced electronic senses has more psychic reality than actual events in the so-called real world and thus shapes political reality more reliably than physical force.

At the climax of Eclipse (yes, it's the first book in a trilogy), the fascist mole crushes the Arc de Triomphe, the very symbol of resistance. But Rickenharp, a rock musician and resistance fighter, has filled the arch's dome with instruments, microphones, and amplifiers, and his final performance to the world via the Grid turns the fascist victory into a symbolic triumph for resistance, which Shirley convincingly demonstrates.

This is about as romantic as neuromantics can get, and it is only fitting that the technical tools of this victory over raw, physical reality are microphones, amplifiers, and electric guitars—the essence of rock 'n' roll. Eclipse may be more of a cyberpunk than a genuine neuromantic novel because "hard science" material is only peripherally addressed, but the absolutely central theme from rock 'n' roll to the climax is characteristic of an original neuromantic sensibility. Because Rickenharp's triumph at the end is a cyborg triumph made possible only through electronic amplification of his fleshly musicality, it clearly demonstrates that cyborgs, romantic cyborgs, Neuromantic cyborgs, use technological enhancement for transcendental purposes since the day Dylan picked up the electric guitar.

As for typical music of our time, we have all accepted neuromancy as given for a quarter century. Eclipse goes further. Rickenharp consumes a drug that enhances his musical creativity, albeit at physical cost. A red thread runs through the entire book: the connection between electronic amplification and transformation in chemically-stimulated humans, showing us what we already know: chemical consciousness alteration is also part of technology, it has long penetrated our whole society, and it is no coincidence that drugs and rock are so closely intertwined. Electronic amplification and consciousness-altering drugs have changed the parameters of human sensing and thus the definition of human perception and psyche.

Other neuromantics take technological changes in our definitions of humanity even further. It's not traditional mistrust against change, but acceptance of technological evolution and modification of our humanity that ultimately defines the neuromantic sensibility. Greg Bear, who pondered what a decent "hard science" type like himself would have lost among cyberpunks at that forum in Austin, unintentionally answered his own question when he left all present bewildered with the most radical neuromantic statement of the entire event.

"How many of you believe that within fifty years people will be clearly recognizable as humans?" he asked the audience. A forest of raised hands. "You're all wrong," Bear explained with his usual calmness. In the short story version of Bear's "Blood Music", an experiment with biochips, meaning with DNA as a read-write computer memory, fails. The altered molecules become conscious and the consciousness rises to a precellular level. These 'noocytes' spread like a disease and infect the population. Humans develop (or rather degenerate) into colony-forming organisms, each molecule endowed with human intelligence. Noocytes dissolve humanity ultimately into its composite molecules, thus erasing humanity, replaced by a new intelligent life form. A sort of scientific horror story. As Bear expanded the short story "Blood Music" into a novel, he became philosophical and a neo-romanticist. While the short story ends with the replacement of humanity by noocytes, the novel continues exploring the new noocyte world. The personalities of the vanished humans are duplicated, multiplied and stored at the noocyte level, preserved by noocytes as obscure heritage and thus made immortal in multiple ways. We witness how a girl's intelligence is enhanced through the translation of her personality into the 'noosphere'. The noosphere itself becomes sort of a transcendental software reality, perhaps a realm of illusion but also a higher state of existence, ultimately detached from the physical universe via the instruments of science and technology. Of course, even "Blood Music" is not the contemporary work that most fully expresses the neo-romantic ethos. While Bear lets human evolution converge into a transcendental endpoint, where humanity is replaced by a higher consciousness that eventually transcends the physical universe, this idea has many forerunners, starting with Olaf Stapledon and Arthur C. Clarke's "Childhood's End" up to Hindu and Buddhist scriptures. Bruce Sterling's novel "Schismatrix", on the other hand, takes different paths and in its own way disrupts our familiar definitions of humanity even more radically. "Schismatrix" is the somewhat picaresque tale of diplomat and rogue Abelard Lindsay, who wanders through space and time in the solar system. From a space colony in lunar orbit to the asteroid belt and outer satellites, Lindsay traverses a series of completely artificial worlds and at the same time imparts profound insights into a good portion of history to the reader. The historical dynamics of Sterling's solar system is driven by the long, sometimes hostile, sometimes mutually penetrating dialectic between 'Mechs' and 'Shapers'. The 'Mechanics' practice cyborg technology while the 'Formers' deal with genetic manipulation and biological transformation. Their endless conflicts are occasionally fought militarily, mostly however economically, diplomatically, technologically and aesthetically, and as success and defeat alternate on both sides, streams of deviants and refugees like Lindsay shift back and forth between the fronts. What ultimately crystallizes out of all this is the schism of the title, a solar system of bewildering human complexity, where 'posthuman' and 'cladogenetic' play key roles. The posthuman is nothing more than the description of the situation that results after decades of genetic manipulation, cyborg practice, cloning and the combination of both species-altering technological techniques. The original human form has been so permanently altered by these technologies that it essentially only occurs in a colony on the moon orbit, set up as some sort of nature reserve. Posthumans do not view this with horror but purely positively. The cladogenetic development is the most extreme current statement of the neo-romantic concept of human evolution through science and technology. In Bear's work, humanity first becomes a single posthuman physical form that then transcends the physical universe. Sterling, on the other hand, introduces the concept of evolutionary diversity through technology. Evolution à la Sterling proceeds cladogenetically or via daughter species, not linearly; it spreads out radially. Successful species do not evolve in a straight line into a single daughter genus but spread out radially into a variety of follow-up species. The fully developed schism contains a complex variety of posthuman species, all the result of natural selection as well as technical development. There are 'Lobsters' that have been so integrated with cyborg technology that they can't tolerate any atmosphere. There are humans biologically adapted to methane seas. An entire space colony has as its interior decoration the altered protoplasm of a single woman, Lindsay's beloved, who retains her human personality. "Schismatrix" is a thoroughly worked-out 'hard science fiction' novel where scientific and technical extrapolations and descriptions of space dwellers are executed with care and detail, something that Heinleins, Nives or even Benfords would be proud of. But while the prose follows the direct, transparent line we expect from the 'hard science' genre, Sterling uses it to equip his novel with equal psychological depth and detail. The characters, especially Lindsay, are convincingly human-psychologically portrayed despite their bizarre physiognomy. And that is perhaps what makes "Schismatrix" even more radical and disturbing than "Blood Music". While Bear's only 'daughter species', the noosphere, is a mystical, transcendental endpoint that has its forerunners in Stapledon, Clarke and most directly in Teilhard de Chardin, Sterling's non-transcendental portrayal of humanity confronts us.

Только выход из относительной нормальности и неуловимого человеческого психа физически переработанного, кладогенетического человечества с неизбежным преобразованием нашего понятия о теле благодаря науке и технике. Странно, но именно Стерлинг, а не Берр, в наименьшей степени находится на этом распутье, двигаясь в направлении, указанном Берром в Остине. Потому что люди, лучше говоря, народы в шизматрии, которые хотя и психически являются нашими братьями и сородичами, физически совсем не похожи на людей. Благодаря науке и технике мы встретим инопланетян, и это будем мы сами. Только в самом конце Стерлинг предлагает некий трансцендентный выход, который трётся о четко очерченную структуру, которую он так тщательно построил, делая Линдси эволюционным конечным пунктом, что не совсем убеждает нас, читателей, и, возможно, даже самого Стерлинга. В целом роман так структурирован, что эволюция нашего вида с помощью науки и техники не имеет конца, а является бесконечно разветвленным процессом. Таким образом, нейромантики сами - по крайней мере на этом этапе - опасаются последних последствий исследования границ технически управляемой человеческой эволюции. Даже видящие нашей расы в настоящее время не способны преодолеть этот порог. Эволюция идет кладогенетически. Точно так же и научная фантастика. Возможно, всегда будет порог, который невозможно преодолеть без помощи потомственной расы.

